# token-saviour

Before acting, identify which layer the task stresses.

## Code reading
Use Serena MCP first for:
- finding symbols
- understanding callers/call paths
- understanding architecture
- semantic code navigation
- semantic edits/renames

Avoid reading many files with cat/grep when Serena can answer.

## Command output
Use RTK for:
- git
- tests
- builds
- grep/find
- kubectl
- other noisy shell commands

## Generated prose
Use Caveman-style concise responses for explanations and summaries.

Do NOT use prose compression on code, configuration, errors, commands, or structured output.

## Generated code
Follow Ponytail principles:
- implement only what was requested
- prefer existing project patterns
- don't introduce abstractions without need
- don't add unnecessary files
- don't add speculative error handling
- don't refactor unrelated code

## Routing rules
Do not use multiple tools for the same layer unnecessarily.

Prefer:
Serena → code understanding
RTK → command output
Caveman → prose
Ponytail → implementation discipline

If a tool is unavailable, fall back to the normal Cursor tool.

Keep responses concise and report which token-saving tool/layer was used when relevant.
